@inherits LocalizedPage

@* An [i] beside a field label that opens its explanation on click.

   For the few settings whose name cannot carry what the reader needs to know. Helper text under the
   field says the same thing, but it is always on screen: two long helper texts in a row of five
   fields pushed their two fields a line higher than the rest, which is what this replaces.

   Click, not hover: a hover-only explanation is unreachable from a touch screen and awkward from a
   keyboard. FlarePopover does both from one Trigger, so no behaviour is hand-rolled here.

   Two things about FlarePopover that cost a measurement each:
   - its Click trigger drives the Open PARAMETER, so without @bind-Open nothing opens, however
     correct the markup looks;
   - the trigger it wraps AnchorContent in is already role="button" with its own tab stop, so the
     anchor must be the icon itself. A FlareIconButton in there nests a button inside a button and
     stops the click reaching the trigger. The name the trigger then lacks comes from the
     visually-hidden label beside the icon. *@
<FlarePopover @bind-Open="_open" Trigger="PopoverTrigger.Click" Placement="Placement.BottomStart" MaxWidth="24rem" Arrow="true">
    <AnchorContent>
        <FlareIconView Value="@MaterialDesign3Icons.Regular.Info" Size="1rem" Color="FlareColor.OnSurfaceVariant" />
        <span class="flare-visually-hidden">@AdminStrings.Common_MoreInfo</span>
    </AnchorContent>
    <ChildContent>
        <FlareText Typo="TypographyScale.BodySmall">@Text</FlareText>
    </ChildContent>
</FlarePopover>

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether the explanation is showing. Bound, because the Click trigger drives it.</summary>
    private bool _open;

    /// <summary>The explanation to show. The same words the field used to carry as helper text.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Text { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
